// Collectible cards (Round 24, Eddie 2026-11-18): every modeled
// fighter gets ONE pre-made collectible card — painted artwork of a
// LORE MOMENT (a vista, an element display, a signature scene, a
// battle beat — not all fight poses), generated from the fighter's
// own portrait as the identity reference so it stays on-model.
// Prime Zero and Griffin have no 2D portrait; their cards were
// painted from still renders of their actual GLBs — no portrait was
// invented for either of them.
//
// Round 25 (Eddie, 2026-11-18) completed the set: the remaining
// designed fighters got their cards the same way — portrait as the
// identity reference where one exists, the collection file's own
// design line where the fighter is text-only — so all 115 designs
// (plus Griffin, who fights from the bar loop) carry exactly one
// card each. The unmodeled fighters' cards are artwork only: they
// grant no model, no toggle, no stage presence anywhere.
//
// Every artwork lives on ONE standard canvas: the real trading-card
// aspect, 2.5 × 3.5 in (750×1050 px), so the digital art transfers
// 1:1 to a future physical print. The generation returned exactly
// 5:7 for all 29 and the canvas pass resized straight to 750×1050 —
// no fighter was cropped to fit, ever (Eddie's never-crop law: the
// fix is always on the asset side, never on the character).
//
// The card has two jobs in the app:
//  1. The Navidex Card view's picture (NavitarDesign, opt-in via
//     the `collectibleArt` prop NavitarShowcase passes) — VISUAL
//     ONLY; stats and lore stay live in the card's data box.
//  2. The SUMMON visual: wherever a 3D model loads (Navidex Stage,
//     card 3D toggle, AR view, battle arena), the card shows first
//     — a light image, instant even on a bad connection — and the
//     model swaps in when it lands. (Round 36.5: the dressed
//     SummonCard composite that used to render it is deleted —
//     the Stage's frame IS the real card now, and ModelViewer
//     shows this bare card art at full size while loading.)
//
// Art is artwork: nothing here claims to be a 3D model, and a
// fighter without a card simply keeps its portrait — the map is
// the whole system, keyed by lowercase fighter name like
// NAVITAR_ART in utils/navitarArt.ts.

const card = (id: string) => `${process.env.PUBLIC_URL}/cards/${id}.webp`;

